---
import ModellingLayout from '@/layouts/ModellingLayout.astro';
---

<ModellingLayout title="Review a definition" description="Test what a definition includes, excludes and leaves ambiguous. Compare plausible revisions, then use an ordinary case and a counterexample to explain what should change." proseClass="prose wide modelling-document learning-document">
  <section aria-labelledby="read">
    <h2 id="read">Read the claim before judging the words</h2>
    <p>A fluent sentence can still define the wrong thing. Read a proposed definition as if you had to use it on an unfamiliar case: what would you include, what would you exclude and what would you need to ask? Your task is to examine the agreement it proposes, not just to improve its style.</p>
    <p>First identify the page's standing and scope. A teaching example invites practice; a candidate term proposes meaning for review; a historical definition records earlier work. Keep the page and candidate version with your note. An attractive definition on a review website does not thereby become an accepted industry definition.</p>
    <p>Then name the subject. Is the wording about a dwelling, an inspection event, a report version, someone's statement or a relationship? If it switches subjects halfway through, a reader may receive the wrong answer while every individual word still looks familiar.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="exercise">
    <h2 id="exercise">The date example: three plausible responses</h2>
    <figure class="learning-specimen" aria-labelledby="draft-caption">
      <figcaption id="draft-caption">Deliberately flawed teaching definition — not a current candidate term</figcaption>
      <blockquote><p>Survey date: the date on which the property was surveyed or the survey report was last issued.</p></blockquote>
      <p>In the fictional Harbour Court case, Flat 1 was inspected on 12 August 2026. Report version 1 was issued on 14 August. Version 2 was issued on 20 August, correcting the access-limitation description without another visit.</p>
    </figure>
    <p>Write a question in your own notes; then compare the responses below. Start with the consequence: “Could the receiver think a new inspection happened?” This is a worked exercise, not a scored test or a submission.</p>
    <h3>“Keep the most recent date”</h3>
    <p>This is attractive when someone wants a single sortable value. But it answers “Which relevant date is latest?” rather than “When did the inspection occur?” The value 20 August could make the visit appear more recent than it was. A selection rule cannot repair a definition that combines different subjects.</p>
    <h3>“Separate occurrence from issue”</h3>
    <p>This addresses the demonstrated ambiguity. “Inspection occurrence date” concerns the event; “report issue date” concerns the identified report version. The receiver can retain both and answer the appropriate question. That is a semantic clarification, not a demand for a particular pair of database fields.</p>
    <h3>“Keep one date element but identify its meaning”</h3>
    <p>A qualified date description could also preserve the distinction if it explicitly identifies whether it concerns inspection occurrence or report issue, and which subject it describes. Keeping the ambiguous definition and relying on a nearby free-text hint would not be enough. The important question is what a recipient can reliably distinguish, not whether the eventual representation has one element or two.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="revision">
    <h2 id="revision">A repair needs a second test</h2>
    <div class="learning-spread">
      <section class="learning-specimen" aria-labelledby="inspection-definition">
        <h3 id="inspection-definition">Illustrative revision: inspection date</h3>
        <blockquote><p>The date on which the identified inspection occurred.</p></blockquote>
        <p>It separates the event from its report. It may still exclude an inspection spanning several days.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="learning-specimen" aria-labelledby="report-definition">
        <h3 id="report-definition">Illustrative revision: report issue date</h3>
        <blockquote><p>The date on which the identified version of the report was issued.</p></blockquote>
        <p>It ties the date to a version. It does not silently make a later recording timestamp the issue date.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <p>If the inspection spans two days, that is a genuine counterexample to a single-day assumption, not a reason to undo the useful event/report distinction. Ask whether the agreed scope needs an occurrence period and what establishes its boundaries. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/explore/dates-periods-and-applicability">dates and periods chapter</a> explains why an interval and its duration answer different questions.</p>
    <p>Changes in wording can be small while changes in meaning are large. Replacing “issued” with “entered into our system” introduces a different event and an organisation-specific setting. Conversely, adding an alternative label need not change the defined meaning at all. State which kind of edit you are proposing.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="questions">
    <h2 id="questions">Look beyond mixed dates</h2>
    <table class="learning-table">
      <caption>Other fictional definitions and the questions they invite</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Proposed wording</th><th scope="col">Problem to test</th><th scope="col">Useful challenge</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">“A verified statement is a statement that has been verified.”</th><td>Circular wording leaves the assessment unnamed.</td><td>What was assessed, against which criteria, by whom and for which purpose? Do not substitute “correct” without evidence.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">“An accessible area is an area the inspector can enter.”</th><td>A hidden setting: general capability, permission or actual physical access during a particular visit?</td><td>Would a normally accessible room count when it was locked during this visit? Name the intended circumstance.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">“A report is current for six months after issue.”</th><td>A local convention is presented as a general applicability rule.</td><td>Which source, purpose and scope justify six months? An internal practice alone does not establish an industry-wide period.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Do not “fix” the last sentence by choosing another duration. A plausible revision might say that a particular organisation assesses a report as suitable for a specified purpose and period. Another might define a profile requirement with a justified time limit. These are different proposals; neither follows merely from a familiar use of “current”.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="learning-practice" aria-labelledby="definition-practice">
    <h2 id="definition-practice">Try it: does “complete” include too much?</h2>
    <p>A fictional draft says: “A complete report is one containing every section in template T.” A reviewer proposes: “A complete report is one that fully describes the property's condition.” The supplied evidence is only template T and a report with all its sections filled in.</p>
    <h3>A reasoned response</h3>
    <p>The original definition is narrow but testable against its stated template. The revision is broader and makes a much stronger claim. A report can contain every section yet describe only an agreed inspection scope. The supplied evidence does not establish that it fully describes every aspect of the property's condition.</p>
    <p>A defensible clarification is “Template-complete report: a report containing the sections required by the identified version of template T.” That keeps documentary completeness separate from completeness or accuracy of knowledge about the property. If the actual need is adequate coverage for a purpose, bring that as a separate question with relevant evidence.</p>
    <p>The ordinary case is the stipulated report containing every required section. A counterexample is a section headed “Roof” saying that roof access was outside the agreed scope: the document can be template-complete without claiming that the roof was inspected.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="prepare">
    <h2 id="prepare">Prepare a contribution somebody can act on</h2>
    <figure class="learning-specimen" aria-labelledby="feedback-caption">
      <figcaption id="feedback-caption">A worked feedback note — fictional and not submitted</figcaption>
      <blockquote><p>The teaching definition “Survey date” combines inspection occurrence with report issue. For Flat 1, the inspection was on 12 August and the corrected report was issued on 20 August without another visit. A recipient could mistake a correction for a more recent inspection. Please distinguish the meanings and test the multi-day case. The example is invented; it is not an approved professional definition.</p></blockquote>
    </figure>
    <p>Replacement wording is optional. The subject, practical consequence and smallest useful counterexample are already a contribution. If you propose wording, explain what it changes and what remains undecided. Link related dictionary or diagram content if the same ambiguity appears there, so a correction does not leave two incompatible views.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="share">
    <h2 id="share">Use the current discussion route</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/development/working-groups/member-guide/teams-and-discussions">Teams and discussion guidance</a> explains group discussion and page-specific comments where available to authorised participants. Keep the reference and related replies together. This reading exercise submits nothing and records no vote.</p>
    <p>Do not paste customer information, live transaction details or restricted evidence into a comment. If a source is needed, <a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute/bring-evidence">prepare its context and permitted handling</a>. Posting feedback does not approve a term, candidate or release.</p>
  </section>
</ModellingLayout>
